</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">We prove theoretically that the GRPO RL algorithm induces a non-trivial process reward model (PRM), under certain assumptions regarding within-group overlap of token sequences across completions. We then show empirically that these assumptions are met under real-world conditions: GRPO does in fact induce a non-trivial PRM. Leveraging the framework of GRPO-as-a-PRM, we identify a flaw in the GRPO objective: non-uniformly distributed process steps hinder both exploration and exploitation (under different conditions). We propose a simple modification to the algorithm to mitigate this defect (<math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>-GRPO), and show that LLMs trained with <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>-GRPO achieve higher validation accuracy and performance on downstream reasoning tasks—and reach peak performance more rapidly—than LLMs trained with standard GRPO. Our results call into question the advantage of costly, explicitly-defined PRMs for GRPO: we show that it is possible to instead leverage the hidden, built-in PRM structure within the vanilla GRPO algorithm to boost model performance with a negligible impact on training time and cost.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="p1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_top">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:0.0pt;height:24.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"></span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Process reward models (PRMs)—models that assign reward to intermediate steps (see Section <a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2 Process Reward Models (PRMs) ‣ 2 Background ‣ GRPO is Secretly a Process Reward Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>)—allow for finer-grained reward assignment than outcome-level signals, thereby yielding improved multi-step reasoning performance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lightman et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. PRMs are therefore particularly applicable to RL training for step-by-step processes such as mathematical reasoning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, training neural PRMs requires costly, step-level human annotation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, and such models are particularly susceptible to reward hacking <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cui et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. These shortcomings have resulted in the limited adoption of learned PRMs for RL training <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Setlur et al. (2025)</a></cite>, leading to the development of Monte-Carlo-based and other heuristic, non-neural PRMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kazemnejad et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hou et al., 2025</a>, e.g.)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">PRMs are typically employed with RL algorithms such as Proximal Policy Optimization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schulman et al., 2017</a>, PPO;)</cite> that employ a critic model and/or generalized advantage estimation (GAE). Although Group Relative Policy Optimization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2024</a>, GRPO;)</cite> greatly simplifies and reduces the memory consumption of RL training, leading to its adoption for a wide range of applications—e.g. tool use <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qian et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sullivan et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, RLHF <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>, and, in particular, mathematical reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">DeepSeek-AI, 2025</a>)</cite>—it does so by eliminating the critic model and GAE of PPO (see Section <a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1 GRPO ‣ 2 Background ‣ GRPO is Secretly a Process Reward Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>). GRPO has therefore not been widely used with PRMs: to the best of our knowledge, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al. (2024)</a></cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. (2025a)</a></cite>, and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Feng et al. (2025)</a></cite> are the only instances in which GRPO is employed with step-level rewards—and these approaches necessitate the modification of the algorithm to accommodate finer-grained reward signals.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we show that—under certain mild assumptions—GRPO induces a Monte-Carlo-based PRM (Section <a href="#S3" title="3 The PRM in the Machine ‣ GRPO is Secretly a Process Reward Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>). Specifically, we prove theoretically that GRPO assigns rewards (and advantages) derived from outcome-level rewards and Monte-Carlo-sampled completions to sub-trajectories, whenever subsets of trajectories within each group share identical prefixes. We then show empirically that this identical-prefix condition is almost always met under real-world conditions, yielding rich step-level process reward structures. These two findings definitively demonstrate that the GRPO objective covertly assigns and optimizes for complex, structured step-level rewards and advantages.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">An investigation of the properties of GRPO’s hidden PRM reveals a defect in the objective function that hinders both exploration and exploitation (under different conditions) during RL training (Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Proposed Approach: 𝜆-GRPO ‣ GRPO is Secretly a Process Reward Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>): namely, a vulnerability to non-uniformly-distributed process steps within a group. To mitigate this shortcoming, we propose including a PRM-aware normalization factor into the GRPO loss function (<math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>-GRPO).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We show that <math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>-GRPO results in higher validation accuracy and a <math id="S1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>2x training speedup over standard GRPO (Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Experiments ‣ GRPO is Secretly a Process Reward Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>). On downstream reasoning benchmarks, <math id="S1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>-GRPO consistently improves over standard GRPO, demonstrating the superiority of our method.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Our findings call into question the utility of dedicated PRMs for GRPO, and suggest that future work may benefit instead from exploiting the step-level reward signal already available to the GRPO algorithm.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Background</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>GRPO</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">GRPO is a variant of PPO that discards the critic model and GAE of the latter. Instead, for each query <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> in the training set, GRPO nondeterministically samples a <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">group</span> <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔾</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{G}}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> trajectories (completions to <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>) and computes the advantage <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{a}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{a}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for the completion <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{(i)}\in{\mathbb{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝔾</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{(i)}\in{\mathbb{G}}</annotation></semantics></math> relative to the mean (outcome) reward of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔾</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{G}}</annotation></semantics></math>, as in Equation <a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2.1 GRPO ‣ 2 Background ‣ GRPO is Secretly a Process Reward Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> (where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{r}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{r}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the reward for <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{a}_{i}=\frac{{r}_{i}-r_{\text{{mean}}}({\mathbb{G}})}{r_{\text{{std}}}({\mathbb{G}})}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">mean</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">std</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{a}_{i}=\frac{{r}_{i}-r_{\text{{mean}}}({\mathbb{G}})}{r_{\text{{std}}}({\mathbb{G}})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In our theoretical analysis in Section <a href="#S3" title="3 The PRM in the Machine ‣ GRPO is Secretly a Process Reward Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we make two key assumptions: first, we assume the use of the DAPO token-level policy gradient objective <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, rather than sample-level loss. Although it differs from the original GRPO formulation laid out in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al. (2024)</a></cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al. (2025)</a></cite> show that this objective leads to more stable training, and it is the standard GRPO loss function employed in commonly-used RL packages (e.g. the TRL GRPO trainer<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
            <a href="https://huggingface.co/docs/trl/main/en/grpo_trainer" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">https://huggingface.co/docs/trl/main/en/grpo_trainer</a></span></span></span>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Second, we assume that the number of update iterations per batch (<math id="S2.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>) is set to <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Under this assumption, the ratio <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{P}_{i,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{P}_{i,t}</annotation></semantics></math> is fixed at <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.0</annotation></semantics></math> (see Equation <a href="#S2.E2.2" title="In 2.1 GRPO ‣ 2 Background ‣ GRPO is Secretly a Process Reward Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2b</span></a>), allowing us to ignore the clipping factor of the GRPO loss function in our theoretical analysis.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Under these two assumptions, the per-group GRPO loss <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{GRPO}}({\mathbb{G}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>GRPO</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{GRPO}}({\mathbb{G}})</annotation></semantics></math> reduces to that in Equation <a href="#S2.E2" title="In 2.1 GRPO ‣ 2 Background ‣ GRPO is Secretly a Process Reward Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody><tr id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_eqn_row"><td class="ltx_eqn_cell" colspan="4"></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.E2.1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle L_{\text{GRPO}}({\mathbb{G}})=\frac{1}{\sum_{g^{(i)}\in{\mathbb{G}}}\textit{len}(g^{(i)})}\sum_{g^{(i)}\in{\mathbb{G}}}\sum_{t=0}^{\textit{len}(g^{(i)})-1}({P}_{i,t}\cdot{a}_{i})-{D}_{i,t}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>GRPO</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝔾</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">len</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝔾</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">len</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle L_{\text{GRPO}}({\mathbb{G}})=\frac{1}{\sum_{g^{(i)}\in{\mathbb{G}}}\textit{len}(g^{(i)})}\sum_{g^{(i)}\in{\mathbb{G}}}\sum_{t=0}^{\textit{len}(g^{(i)})-1}({P}_{i,t}\cdot{a}_{i})-{D}_{i,t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2a)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S2.E2.2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{P}_{i,t}=\frac{\pi_{\theta}(g^{(i)}_{t}\hskip 2.84526pt|\hskip 2.84526ptq,g^{(i)}_{:t})}{\pi_{\theta_{\textit{old}}}(g^{(i)}_{t}\hskip 2.84526pt|\hskip 2.84526ptq,g^{(i)}_{:t})}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.447em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">old</mtext></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.447em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{P}_{i,t}=\frac{\pi_{\theta}(g^{(i)}_{t}\hskip 2.84526pt|\hskip 2.84526ptq,g^{(i)}_{:t})}{\pi_{\theta_{\textit{old}}}(g^{(i)}_{t}\hskip 2.84526pt|\hskip 2.84526ptq,g^{(i)}_{:t})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2b)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S2.E2.3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{D}_{i,t}=\frac{\pi_{\theta_{\textit{ref}}}(g^{(i)}_{t}\hskip 2.84526pt|\hskip 2.84526ptq,g^{(i)}_{:t})}{\pi_{\theta}(g^{(i)}_{t}\hskip 2.84526pt|\hskip 2.84526ptq,g^{(i)}_{:t})}-\textit{ln}\frac{\pi_{\theta_{\textit{ref}}}(g^{(i)}_{t}\hskip 2.84526pt|\hskip 2.84526ptq,g^{(i)}_{:t})}{\pi_{\theta}(g^{(i)}_{t}\hskip 2.84526pt|\hskip 2.84526ptq,g^{(i)}_{:t})}-1" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">ref</mtext></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.447em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.447em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">ln</mtext><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">ref</mtext></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.447em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.447em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{D}_{i,t}=\frac{\pi_{\theta_{\textit{ref}}}(g^{(i)}_{t}\hskip 2.84526pt|\hskip 2.84526ptq,g^{(i)}_{:t})}{\pi_{\theta}(g^{(i)}_{t}\hskip 2.84526pt|\hskip 2.84526ptq,g^{(i)}_{:t})}-\textit{ln}\frac{\pi_{\theta_{\textit{ref}}}(g^{(i)}_{t}\hskip 2.84526pt|\hskip 2.84526ptq,g^{(i)}_{:t})}{\pi_{\theta}(g^{(i)}_{t}\hskip 2.84526pt|\hskip 2.84526ptq,g^{(i)}_{:t})}-1</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2c)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Process Reward Models (PRMs)</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given an alphabet <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e. set of tokens), we formally define a PRM as a function <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\phi}\colon\Sigma^{*}\to(\Sigma^{*}\times\mathbb{R})^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\phi}\colon\Sigma^{*}\to(\Sigma^{*}\times\mathbb{R})^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> parameterized by <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> that maps a trajectory <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\Sigma^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\Sigma^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> to the sequence <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f_{\phi}(y)=((y_{:i_{1}},r^{(y)}_{0}),(y_{i_{1}:i_{2}},r^{(y)}_{1}),\dots,(y_{i_{n-1}:},r^{(y)}_{n-1}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\phi}(y)=((y_{:i_{1}},r^{(y)}_{0}),(y_{i_{1}:i_{2}},r^{(y)}_{1}),\dots,(y_{i_{n-1}:},r^{(y)}_{n-1}))</annotation></semantics></math> of pairs of <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">process steps</span> (sub-trajectories) <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="y_{i_{k}:i_{k+1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i_{k}:i_{k+1}}</annotation></semantics></math> and step-level rewards <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{(y)}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{(y)}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">While PRMs are typically contrasted with <span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">outcome</span> reward models (ORMs)—which assign a single reward to the entire trajectory—under the above definition, an ORM <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\phi^{\prime}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\phi^{\prime}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is simply a <span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">trivial</span> PRM: i.e. <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\phi^{\prime}}^{\prime}(y)=((y,r^{(y)}_{0}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\phi^{\prime}}^{\prime}(y)=((y,r^{(y)}_{0}))</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Both the division of the trajectory <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> into steps and the assignment of rewards to those steps are dependent upon the PRM in question. When trajectories are clearly delineated into individual steps—e.g. via ReACT-style prompting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al. (2023)</a></cite> or instructing the model to divide its reasoning into demarcated steps—the PRM can simply be directed to assign a reward to each pre-defined step <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li &amp; Li, 2025</a>, e.g.)</cite>. In other cases, trajectories are heuristically split into steps—for example, at high-entropy tokens <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hou et al., 2025</a>, e.g.)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Although the assignment of step-level reward can be performed by a model with learned parameters <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Uesato et al., 2022</a>, e.g.)</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kazemnejad et al. (2025)</a></cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hou et al. (2025)</a></cite> combine Monte Carlo estimation with outcome-level rewards to yield heuristic PRMs that do not require the labor-intensive annotation of—and are less susceptible to reward-hacking than—their learned counterparts. In cases such as these in which the PRM <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> is not learned, we simply consider <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> to be fixed/trivial.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>The PRM in the Machine</h2>

<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.21154v1/bg_tree.svg" id="S3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/182;" width="548" height="182"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Toy example of a group <math id="S3.F1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{G}}=\{g^{(1)},\dots,g^{(6)}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔾</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{G}}=\{g^{(1)},\dots,g^{(6)}\}</annotation></semantics></math> (left) and its corresponding <math id="S3.F1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{B}}({\mathbb{G}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{B}}({\mathbb{G}})</annotation></semantics></math> tree (right).
Tokens (boxes) are numbered for readability—subscripted numbers within boxes only indicate position. Each process <span id="S3.F1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">set</span> (node in the <math id="S3.F1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{B}}({\mathbb{G}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{B}}({\mathbb{G}})</annotation></semantics></math> tree) is a set of trajectories that share a common prefix, and corresponds to a process <span id="S3.F1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">step</span> (subtrajectory) spanning those shared tokens: in this figure, colored nodes in <math id="S3.F1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{B}}({\mathbb{G}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{B}}({\mathbb{G}})</annotation></semantics></math> correspond to those subsequences in <math id="S3.F1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔾</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{G}}</annotation></semantics></math> that span tokens/boxes of the same color<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
            
            
            
          Same-colored boxes in <math id="footnotex1.m1a" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔾</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{G}}</annotation></semantics></math> indicate identical <span id="footnote2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">sequences</span> of tokens across trajectories only: for example, <math id="footnotex1.m2a" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="g^{(3)}_{:4}=g^{(4)}_{:4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>g</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{(3)}_{:4}=g^{(4)}_{:4}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="footnotex1.m3a" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="g^{(4)}_{4:6}=g^{(5)}_{4:6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>g</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{(4)}_{4:6}=g^{(5)}_{4:6}</annotation></semantics></math>, but it is not necessarily the case that e.g. <math id="footnotex1.m4a" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{(3)}_{0}=g^{(3)}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{(3)}_{0}=g^{(3)}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></span>. GRPO implicitly assigns a step-level reward and advantage to the tokens of each process step, which are computed as functions of the mean outcome-level reward of each trajectory in the corresponding process set.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Theoretical Analysis ‣ 3 The PRM in the Machine ‣ GRPO is Secretly a Process Reward Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, we prove that GRPO theoretically induces a PRM (given the assumptions of Section <a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1 GRPO ‣ 2 Background ‣ GRPO is Secretly a Process Reward Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>) as defined in Section <a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2 Process Reward Models (PRMs) ‣ 2 Background ‣ GRPO is Secretly a Process Reward Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>. However, this PRM is only non-trivial—i.e. not equivalent to an ORM—if subsets of trajectories within each group share identical initial sub-trajectories.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Empirical Analysis ‣ 3 The PRM in the Machine ‣ GRPO is Secretly a Process Reward Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, we empirically demonstrate that such rich, overlapping prefix structures arise very frequently under real-world conditions: this shows that GRPO is “secretly” a non-trivial PRM.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Theoretical Analysis</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathcal{B}}({\mathbb{G}})=\{\lambda\subseteq{\mathbb{G}}\hskip 2.84526pt|\hskip 2.84526pt\exists n\geq 0\forall g^{(i)},g^{(k)}\in\lambda\colon g^{(i)}_{:n}=g^{(k)}_{:n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>𝔾</mi><mo fence="false" lspace="0.280em" rspace="0.447em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∃</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{B}}({\mathbb{G}})=\{\lambda\subseteq{\mathbb{G}}\hskip 2.84526pt|\hskip 2.84526pt\exists n\geq 0\forall g^{(i)},g^{(k)}\in\lambda\colon g^{(i)}_{:n}=g^{(k)}_{:n}\}</annotation></semantics></math> be the set of all <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">process sets</span>: sets <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\subseteq{\mathbb{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>𝔾</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\subseteq{\mathbb{G}}</annotation></semantics></math> of completions such that all <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{(i)}\in\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{(i)}\in\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> are identical up to the <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{th}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{th}</annotation></semantics></math> token, for some <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> (see Figure <a href="#footnote2" title="footnote 2 ‣ Figure 1 ‣ 3 The PRM in the Machine ‣ GRPO is Secretly a Process Reward Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). Note that there is a natural tree structure on <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{B}}({\mathbb{G}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{B}}({\mathbb{G}})</annotation></semantics></math>, which is induced by the <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\supseteq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊇</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\supseteq</annotation></semantics></math> relation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Each <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in{\mathcal{B}}({\mathbb{G}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in{\mathcal{B}}({\mathbb{G}})</annotation></semantics></math> defines a process step within each <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{(i)}\in\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{(i)}\in\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, spanning the subsequence <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="g^{(i)}_{s(\lambda):e(\lambda)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{(i)}_{s(\lambda):e(\lambda)}</annotation></semantics></math> from the <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s(\lambda)^{th}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s(\lambda)^{th}</annotation></semantics></math> to the <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e(\lambda)^{th}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e(\lambda)^{th}</annotation></semantics></math> tokens of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math>. The endpoint <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as the largest <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="S3.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="g^{(i)}_{:n}=g^{(k)}_{:n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>g</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{(i)}_{:n}=g^{(k)}_{:n}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{(i)},g^{(k)}\in\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{(i)},g^{(k)}\in\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as the endpoint of the immediate parent <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Pa_{{\mathcal{B}}({\mathbb{G}})}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Pa_{{\mathcal{B}}({\mathbb{G}})}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> in the tree structure induced on <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{B}}({\mathbb{G}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{B}}({\mathbb{G}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}&amp;e(\lambda)=\textit{max}\{n\geq 0\hskip 2.84526pt|\hskip 2.84526pt\forall g^{(i)},g^{(k)}\in\lambda:g^{(i)}_{:n}=g^{(k)}_{:n}\}\\
&amp;s(\lambda)=\begin{cases}0&amp;\text{if }\lambda=\textit{root}(\mathcal{B}(G))\\
e(Pa_{{\mathcal{B}}({\mathbb{G}})}(\lambda))&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">max</mtext><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn><mo fence="false" lspace="0.560em" rspace="0.727em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>g</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>g</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>g</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">root</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>otherwise</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}&amp;e(\lambda)=\textit{max}\{n\geq 0\hskip 2.84526pt|\hskip 2.84526pt\forall g^{(i)},g^{(k)}\in\lambda:g^{(i)}_{:n}=g^{(k)}_{:n}\}\\
&amp;s(\lambda)=\begin{cases}0&amp;\text{if }\lambda=\textit{root}(\mathcal{B}(G))\\
e(Pa_{{\mathcal{B}}({\mathbb{G}})}(\lambda))&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For example, <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s(\{g^{(4)},g^{(5)}\})=e(\{g^{(3)},g^{(4)},g^{(5)}\})=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s(\{g^{(4)},g^{(5)}\})=e(\{g^{(3)},g^{(4)},g^{(5)}\})=4</annotation></semantics></math> in Figure <a href="#footnote2" title="footnote 2 ‣ Figure 1 ‣ 3 The PRM in the Machine ‣ GRPO is Secretly a Process Reward Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, and <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e(\{g^{(4)},g^{(5)}\})=6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e(\{g^{(4)},g^{(5)}\})=6</annotation></semantics></math>: the process step corresponding to <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{g^{(4)},g^{(5)}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{g^{(4)},g^{(5)}\}</annotation></semantics></math> spans <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="g^{(4)}_{4:6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{(4)}_{4:6}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="g^{(5)}_{4:6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{(5)}_{4:6}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">For each <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{(i)}\in{\mathbb{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝔾</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{(i)}\in{\mathbb{G}}</annotation></semantics></math> and each <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq t&lt;\textit{len}(g^{(i)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">len</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq t&lt;\textit{len}(g^{(i)})</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda^{(i,t)}\in{\mathcal{B}}({\mathbb{G}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda^{(i,t)}\in{\mathcal{B}}({\mathbb{G}})</annotation></semantics></math> denote the unique process set such that <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{(i)}\in\lambda^{(i,t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{(i)}\in\lambda^{(i,t)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s(\lambda^{(i,t)})\leq t&lt;e(\lambda^{(i,t)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s(\lambda^{(i,t)})\leq t&lt;e(\lambda^{(i,t)})</annotation></semantics></math>. In other words, <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda^{(i,t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda^{(i,t)}</annotation></semantics></math> is the process step to which the token <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{(i)}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{(i)}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> belongs. In Figure <a href="#footnote2" title="footnote 2 ‣ Figure 1 ‣ 3 The PRM in the Machine ‣ GRPO is Secretly a Process Reward Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda^{(i,t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda^{(i,t)}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to the set whose color matches that of <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{(i)}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{(i)}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda^{(1,0)}=\{g^{(1)},g^{(2)}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda^{(1,0)}=\{g^{(1)},g^{(2)}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda^{(1,3)}=\{g^{(1)}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda^{(1,3)}=\{g^{(1)}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda^{(5,5)}=\{g^{(4)},g^{(5)}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda^{(5,5)}=\{g^{(4)},g^{(5)}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, etc.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Now, for each process step defined by some <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in{\mathcal{B}}({\mathbb{G}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in{\mathcal{B}}({\mathbb{G}})</annotation></semantics></math>, we define the step-level reward <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{R}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{R}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> via Monte Carlo estimation (Equation <a href="#S3.E3" title="In 3.1 Theoretical Analysis ‣ 3 The PRM in the Machine ‣ GRPO is Secretly a Process Reward Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>): <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{R}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{R}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> is the mean outcome-level reward of each trajectory in <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>. In other words, <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{R}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{R}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> is the mean reward of each leaf node dominated by <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> in the tree structure induced on <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{B}}({\mathbb{G}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{B}}({\mathbb{G}})</annotation></semantics></math>—i.e. of each sampled completion to the process step defined by <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{R}(\lambda)=\frac{\sum_{g^{(i)}\in\lambda}{r}_{i}}{|\lambda|}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{R}(\lambda)=\frac{\sum_{g^{(i)}\in\lambda}{r}_{i}}{|\lambda|}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">For each trajectory <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{(i)}\in{\mathbb{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝔾</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{(i)}\in{\mathbb{G}}</annotation></semantics></math> and each <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq t&lt;\textit{len}(g^{(i)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">len</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq t&lt;\textit{len}(g^{(i)})</annotation></semantics></math> define the reward <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{R}_{i,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{R}_{i,t}</annotation></semantics></math> for the token <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{(i)}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{(i)}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> as the reward of the process step to which <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{(i)}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{(i)}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> belongs: <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{R}_{i,t}=\hat{R}(\lambda^{(i,t)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{R}_{i,t}=\hat{R}(\lambda^{(i,t)})</annotation></semantics></math>. For example, in Figure <a href="#footnote2" title="footnote 2 ‣ Figure 1 ‣ 3 The PRM in the Machine ‣ GRPO is Secretly a Process Reward Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, the step-level reward for the sub-trajectories <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="g^{(3)}_{:4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{(3)}_{:4}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="g^{(4)}_{:4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{(4)}_{:4}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="g^{(5)}_{:4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{(5)}_{:4}</annotation></semantics></math> is the mean of the outcome-level rewards for <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{(3)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{(3)}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{(4)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{(4)}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{(5)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{(5)}</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{R}_{3,0}=\dots={R}_{5,3}=\textit{mean}(\{{r}_{3},{r}_{4},{r}_{5}\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">mean</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{R}_{3,0}=\dots={R}_{5,3}=\textit{mean}(\{{r}_{3},{r}_{4},{r}_{5}\})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">By the definition given in Section <a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2 Process Reward Models (PRMs) ‣ 2 Background ‣ GRPO is Secretly a Process Reward Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>, <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{R}_{i,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{R}_{i,t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{B}}({\mathbb{G}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{B}}({\mathbb{G}})</annotation></semantics></math> clearly define a PRM: each <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{(i)}\in{\mathbb{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝔾</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{(i)}\in{\mathbb{G}}</annotation></semantics></math> is mapped to the sequence <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(g^{(i)}_{:s(\lambda_{1})},{R}_{i,0}),(g^{(i)}_{s(\lambda_{1}):s(\lambda_{2})},{R}_{i,s(\lambda_{1})}),\dots,(g^{(i)}_{s(\lambda_{n}):},{R}_{i,s(\lambda_{n})})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g^{(i)}_{:s(\lambda_{1})},{R}_{i,0}),(g^{(i)}_{s(\lambda_{1}):s(\lambda_{2})},{R}_{i,s(\lambda_{1})}),\dots,(g^{(i)}_{s(\lambda_{n}):},{R}_{i,s(\lambda_{n})})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\lambda_{0}={\mathbb{G}})\rightarrow\dots\rightarrow(\lambda_{n}=\{g^{(i)}\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔾</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\lambda_{0}={\mathbb{G}})\rightarrow\dots\rightarrow(\lambda_{n}=\{g^{(i)}\})</annotation></semantics></math> is the unique path in the tree structure induced on <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{B}}({\mathbb{G}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{B}}({\mathbb{G}})</annotation></semantics></math> from the root <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔾</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{G}}</annotation></semantics></math> to the node <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{g^{(i)}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{g^{(i)}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Now, define the step-level advantage <math id="S3.SS1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{A}_{i,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{A}_{i,t}</annotation></semantics></math> for the token <math id="S3.SS1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{(i)}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{(i)}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> in an analogous manner to the original GRPO definition in Equation <a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2.1 GRPO ‣ 2 Background ‣ GRPO is Secretly a Process Reward Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>—i.e. as the normalized difference between the step-level reward <math id="S3.SS1.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{R}_{i,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{R}_{i,t}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS1.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{(i)}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{(i)}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and the mean reward of <math id="S3.SS1.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔾</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{G}}</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S3.SS1.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{A}_{i,t}=({R}_{i,t}-r_{\textit{mean}}({\mathbb{G}}))/r_{\textit{std}}({\mathbb{G}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">mean</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">std</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{A}_{i,t}=({R}_{i,t}-r_{\textit{mean}}({\mathbb{G}}))/r_{\textit{std}}({\mathbb{G}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p11" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Replacing the term <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{a}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{a}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{A}_{i,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{A}_{i,t}</annotation></semantics></math> in Equation <a href="#S2.E2.1" title="In 2 ‣ 2.1 GRPO ‣ 2 Background ‣ GRPO is Secretly a Process Reward Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2a</span></a> yields a PRM-aware RL objective (Equation <a href="#S3.E4" title="In 3.1 Theoretical Analysis ‣ 3 The PRM in the Machine ‣ GRPO is Secretly a Process Reward Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p12" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{PRM}}({\mathbb{G}})=\frac{1}{\sum_{g^{(i)}\in{\mathbb{G}}}\textit{len}(g^{(i)})}\sum_{g^{(i)}\in{\mathbb{G}}}\sum_{t=0}^{\textit{len}(g^{(i)})-1}({P}_{i,t}\cdot\hbox{\pagecolor{yellow}$\displaystyle{A}_{i,t}$})-{D}_{i,t}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>PRM</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝔾</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">len</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝔾</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">len</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFFF00;" mathbackground="#FFFF00">A</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFFF00;" mathbackground="#FFFF00">i</mi><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFFF00;" mathbackground="#FFFF00">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFFF00;" mathbackground="#FFFF00">t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{PRM}}({\mathbb{G}})=\frac{1}{\sum_{g^{(i)}\in{\mathbb{G}}}\textit{len}(g^{(i)})}\sum_{g^{(i)}\in{\mathbb{G}}}\sum_{t=0}^{\textit{len}(g^{(i)})-1}({P}_{i,t}\cdot\hbox{\pagecolor{yellow}$\displaystyle{A}_{i,t}$})-{D}_{i,t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p13" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p13.1" class="ltx_p">We now show that the standard GRPO objective defined in Equation <a href="#S2.E2.1" title="In 2 ‣ 2.1 GRPO ‣ 2 Background ‣ GRPO is Secretly a Process Reward Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2a</span></a> with outcome-level rewards (<math id="S3.SS1.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{GRPO}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>GRPO</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{GRPO}}</annotation></semantics></math>) is equivalent to the PRM defined in Equations <a href="#S3.E3" title="In 3.1 Theoretical Analysis ‣ 3 The PRM in the Machine ‣ GRPO is Secretly a Process Reward Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>-<a href="#S3.E4" title="In 3.1 Theoretical Analysis ‣ 3 The PRM in the Machine ‣ GRPO is Secretly a Process Reward Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> (<math id="S3.SS1.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{PRM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>PRM</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{PRM}}</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1</span></span><span id="Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any query <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, policy <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, and group <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{G}}\sim\pi_{\theta}(\text{\textendash}\hskip 2.84526pt|\hskip 2.84526ptq)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔾</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">–</mtext><mo>|</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{G}}\sim\pi_{\theta}(\text{\textendash}\hskip 2.84526pt|\hskip 2.84526ptq)</annotation></semantics></math> with outcome-level rewards <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{r_{i}\}_{g^{(i)}\in{\mathbb{G}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝔾</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{r_{i}\}_{g^{(i)}\in{\mathbb{G}}}</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{GRPO}}({\mathbb{G}})=L_{\text{PRM}}({\mathbb{G}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">GRPO</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">PRM</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{GRPO}}({\mathbb{G}})=L_{\text{PRM}}({\mathbb{G}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS1.p14" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S3.SS1.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> be any process set in <math id="S3.SS1.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{B}}({\mathbb{G}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{B}}({\mathbb{G}})</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S3.SS1.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> be any integer such that <math id="S3.SS1.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s(\lambda)\leq t&lt;e(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s(\lambda)\leq t&lt;e(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math>. We first prove that the sum of the PRM loss terms <math id="S3.SS1.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{P}_{i,t}\cdot{A}_{i,t}-{D}_{i,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{P}_{i,t}\cdot{A}_{i,t}-{D}_{i,t}</annotation></semantics></math> for each trajectory <math id="S3.SS1.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{(i)}\in\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{(i)}\in\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> at the token <math id="S3.SS1.p14.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is equivalent to the sum of the standard GRPO loss terms <math id="S3.SS1.p14.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{P}_{i,t}\cdot{a}_{i}-{D}_{i,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{P}_{i,t}\cdot{a}_{i}-{D}_{i,t}</annotation></semantics></math> (Equation <a href="#S3.E5" title="In Proof. ‣ 3.1 Theoretical Analysis ‣ 3 The PRM in the Machine ‣ GRPO is Secretly a Process Reward Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p15" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text">Recall that for any <math id="S3.SS1.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{(i)},g^{(k)}\in\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{(i)},g^{(k)}\in\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p15.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="g^{(i)}_{:t+1}=g^{(k)}_{:t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>g</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{(i)}_{:t+1}=g^{(k)}_{:t+1}</annotation></semantics></math> by definition: therefore, <math id="S3.SS1.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{P}_{i,t}={P}_{k,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{P}_{i,t}={P}_{k,t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{D}_{i,t}={D}_{k,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{D}_{i,t}={D}_{k,t}</annotation></semantics></math> (Equations <a href="#S2.E2.2" title="In 2.1 GRPO ‣ 2 Background ‣ GRPO is Secretly a Process Reward Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2b</span></a>-<a href="#S2.E2.3" title="In 2.1 GRPO ‣ 2 Background ‣ GRPO is Secretly a Process Reward Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2c</span></a>). Again by definition, <math id="S3.SS1.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{(i)},g^{(k)}\in\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{(i)},g^{(k)}\in\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> implies that <math id="S3.SS1.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{R}_{i,t}={R}_{k,t}=\hat{R}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{R}_{i,t}={R}_{k,t}=\hat{R}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> (Equation <a href="#S3.E3" title="In 3.1 Theoretical Analysis ‣ 3 The PRM in the Machine ‣ GRPO is Secretly a Process Reward Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), and so <math id="S3.SS1.p15.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{A}_{i,t}={A}_{k,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{A}_{i,t}={A}_{k,t}</annotation></semantics></math>. As such, we may define <math id="S3.SS1.p15.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{P}_{t}(\lambda)={P}_{k,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{P}_{t}(\lambda)={P}_{k,t}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p15.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{D}_{t}(\lambda)={D}_{k,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{D}_{t}(\lambda)={D}_{k,t}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS1.p15.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{A}(\lambda)={A}_{k,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{A}(\lambda)={A}_{k,t}</annotation></semantics></math> in Equation <a href="#S3.E5" title="In Proof. ‣ 3.1 Theoretical Analysis ‣ 3 The PRM in the Machine ‣ GRPO is Secretly a Process Reward Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, choosing any arbitrary <math id="S3.SS1.p15.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{(k)}\in\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{(k)}\in\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p16" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}&amp;\sum_{g^{(i)}\in\lambda}({P}_{i,t}\cdot{A}_{i,t})-{D}_{i,t}=|\lambda|\cdot((\hat{P}_{t}(\lambda)\cdot\hat{A}(\lambda))-\hat{D}_{t}(\lambda))\\
&amp;=|\lambda|\cdot\left(\left(\hat{P}_{t}(\lambda)\cdot\frac{\hat{R}(\lambda)-r_{\textit{mean}}({\mathbb{G}})}{r_{\textit{std}}({\mathbb{G}})}\right)-\hat{D}_{t}(\lambda)\right)=|\lambda|\cdot\left(\left(\hat{P}_{t}(\lambda)\cdot\frac{\sum_{g^{(i)}\in\lambda}\frac{{r}_{i}}{|\lambda|}-r_{\textit{mean}}({\mathbb{G}})}{r_{\textit{std}}({\mathbb{G}})}\right)-\hat{D}_{t}(\lambda)\right)\\
&amp;=\left(\hat{P}_{t}(\lambda)\frac{|\lambda|(\sum_{g^{(i)}\in\lambda}\frac{{r}_{i}}{|\lambda|}-r_{\textit{mean}}({\mathbb{G}}))}{r_{\textit{std}}({\mathbb{G}})}\right)-|\lambda|\hat{D}_{t}(\lambda)=\left(\hat{P}_{t}(\lambda)\frac{\sum_{g^{(i)}\in\lambda}{r}_{i}-\sum_{g^{(i)}\in\lambda}r_{\textit{mean}}({\mathbb{G}})}{r_{\textit{std}}({\mathbb{G}})}\right)-|\lambda|\hat{D}_{t}(\lambda)\\
&amp;=\left(\sum_{g^{(i)}\in\lambda}{P}_{i,t}\frac{{r}_{i}-r_{\textit{mean}}({\mathbb{G}})}{r_{\textit{std}}({\mathbb{G}})}\right)-\sum_{g^{(i)}\in\lambda}{D}_{i,t}=\sum_{g^{(i)}\in\lambda}({P}_{i,t}\cdot{a}_{i})-{D}_{i,t}\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">mean</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">std</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msub><mfrac><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">mean</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">std</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msub><mfrac><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">mean</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">std</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">mean</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">std</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">mean</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">std</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}&amp;\sum_{g^{(i)}\in\lambda}({P}_{i,t}\cdot{A}_{i,t})-{D}_{i,t}=|\lambda|\cdot((\hat{P}_{t}(\lambda)\cdot\hat{A}(\lambda))-\hat{D}_{t}(\lambda))\\
&amp;=|\lambda|\cdot\left(\left(\hat{P}_{t}(\lambda)\cdot\frac{\hat{R}(\lambda)-r_{\textit{mean}}({\mathbb{G}})}{r_{\textit{std}}({\mathbb{G}})}\right)-\hat{D}_{t}(\lambda)\right)=|\lambda|\cdot\left(\left(\hat{P}_{t}(\lambda)\cdot\frac{\sum_{g^{(i)}\in\lambda}\frac{{r}_{i}}{|\lambda|}-r_{\textit{mean}}({\mathbb{G}})}{r_{\textit{std}}({\mathbb{G}})}\right)-\hat{D}_{t}(\lambda)\right)\\
&amp;=\left(\hat{P}_{t}(\lambda)\frac{|\lambda|(\sum_{g^{(i)}\in\lambda}\frac{{r}_{i}}{|\lambda|}-r_{\textit{mean}}({\mathbb{G}}))}{r_{\textit{std}}({\mathbb{G}})}\right)-|\lambda|\hat{D}_{t}(\lambda)=\left(\hat{P}_{t}(\lambda)\frac{\sum_{g^{(i)}\in\lambda}{r}_{i}-\sum_{g^{(i)}\in\lambda}r_{\textit{mean}}({\mathbb{G}})}{r_{\textit{std}}({\mathbb{G}})}\right)-|\lambda|\hat{D}_{t}(\lambda)\\
&amp;=\left(\sum_{g^{(i)}\in\lambda}{P}_{i,t}\frac{{r}_{i}-r_{\textit{mean}}({\mathbb{G}})}{r_{\textit{std}}({\mathbb{G}})}\right)-\sum_{g^{(i)}\in\lambda}{D}_{i,t}=\sum_{g^{(i)}\in\lambda}({P}_{i,t}\cdot{a}_{i})-{D}_{i,t}\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p17" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p17.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p17.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now, letting <math id="S3.SS1.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\textit{max}}=\textit{max}_{g^{(i)}\in{\mathbb{G}}}\hskip 2.84526pt\textit{len}(g^{(i)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">max</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">max</mtext><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝔾</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">len</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\textit{max}}=\textit{max}_{g^{(i)}\in{\mathbb{G}}}\hskip 2.84526pt\textit{len}(g^{(i)})</annotation></semantics></math>, for each <math id="S3.SS1.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq t&lt;t_{\textit{max}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">max</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq t&lt;t_{\textit{max}}</annotation></semantics></math> we can define a partition <math id="S3.SS1.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}_{t}\subseteq{\mathcal{B}}({\mathbb{G}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝕏</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}_{t}\subseteq{\mathcal{B}}({\mathbb{G}})</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.SS1.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{g^{(i)}\in{\mathbb{G}}\hskip 2.84526pt|\hskip 2.84526pt\textit{len}(g^{(i)})\leq t\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝔾</mi></mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">len</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{g^{(i)}\in{\mathbb{G}}\hskip 2.84526pt|\hskip 2.84526pt\textit{len}(g^{(i)})\leq t\}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.SS1.p17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}_{t}=\{\lambda\in{\mathcal{B}}({\mathbb{G}})\hskip 2.84526pt|\hskip 2.84526pts(\lambda)\leq t&lt;e(\lambda)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝕏</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}_{t}=\{\lambda\in{\mathcal{B}}({\mathbb{G}})\hskip 2.84526pt|\hskip 2.84526pts(\lambda)\leq t&lt;e(\lambda)\}</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of all process sets corresponding to a token span containing the index <math id="S3.SS1.p17.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. The GRPO loss term <math id="S3.SS1.p17.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{GRPO}}({\mathbb{G}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>GRPO</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{GRPO}}({\mathbb{G}})</annotation></semantics></math> (Equation <a href="#S2.E2.1" title="In 2 ‣ 2.1 GRPO ‣ 2 Background ‣ GRPO is Secretly a Process Reward Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2a</span></a>) can be equivalently expressed as in Equation <a href="#S3.E6" title="In Proof. ‣ 3.1 Theoretical Analysis ‣ 3 The PRM in the Machine ‣ GRPO is Secretly a Process Reward Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> (and analogously for <math id="S3.SS1.p17.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{PRM}}({\mathbb{G}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>PRM</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{PRM}}({\mathbb{G}})</annotation></semantics></math> of Equation <a href="#S3.E4" title="In 3.1 Theoretical Analysis ‣ 3 The PRM in the Machine ‣ GRPO is Secretly a Process Reward Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p18" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{GRPO}}({\mathbb{G}})=\frac{1}{\sum_{g^{(i)}\in{\mathbb{G}}}\textit{len}(g^{(i)})}\cdot\sum_{t=0}^{t_{\textit{max}}-1}\sum_{\lambda\in{\mathbb{X}}_{t}}\sum_{g_{i}\in\lambda}({P}_{i,t}\cdot{a}_{i})-{D}_{i,t}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>GRPO</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝔾</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">len</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.055em">⋅</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">max</mtext></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝕏</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{GRPO}}({\mathbb{G}})=\frac{1}{\sum_{g^{(i)}\in{\mathbb{G}}}\textit{len}(g^{(i)})}\cdot\sum_{t=0}^{t_{\textit{max}}-1}\sum_{\lambda\in{\mathbb{X}}_{t}}\sum_{g_{i}\in\lambda}({P}_{i,t}\cdot{a}_{i})-{D}_{i,t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p19" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p19.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p19.1.1" class="ltx_text">We then have the following equalities by Equations <a href="#S3.E5" title="In Proof. ‣ 3.1 Theoretical Analysis ‣ 3 The PRM in the Machine ‣ GRPO is Secretly a Process Reward Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> and <a href="#S3.E6" title="In Proof. ‣ 3.1 Theoretical Analysis ‣ 3 The PRM in the Machine ‣ GRPO is Secretly a Process Reward Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p20" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}L_{\text{GRPO}}({\mathbb{G}})&amp;=\frac{1}{\sum_{g^{(i)}\in{\mathbb{G}}}\textit{len}(g^{(i)})}\cdot\sum_{g^{(i)}\in{\mathbb{G}}}\sum_{t=0}^{\textit{len}(g^{(i)})-1}({P}_{i,t}\cdot{a}_{i})-{D}_{i,t}=\\
\frac{1}{\sum_{g^{(i)}\in{\mathbb{G}}}\textit{len}(g^{(i)})}\cdot\sum_{t=0}^{t_{\textit{max}}-1}&amp;\sum_{\lambda\in{\mathbb{X}}_{t}}\sum_{g^{(i)}\in\lambda}({P}_{i,t}\cdot{a}_{i})-{D}_{i,t}=\frac{1}{\sum_{g^{(i)}\in{\mathbb{G}}}\textit{len}(g^{(i)})}\cdot\sum_{t=0}^{t_{\textit{max}}-1}\sum_{\lambda\in{\mathbb{X}}_{t}}\sum_{g^{(i)}\in\lambda}({P}_{i,t}\cdot{A}_{i,t})-{D}_{i,t}\\
&amp;=\frac{1}{\sum_{g^{(i)}\in{\mathbb{G}}}\textit{len}(g^{(i)})}\cdot\sum_{g^{(i)}\in{\mathbb{G}}}\sum_{t=0}^{\textit{len}(g^{(i)})-1}({P}_{i,t}\cdot{A}_{i,t})-{D}_{i,t}=L_{\text{PRM}}({\mathbb{G}})\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>GRPO</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝔾</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">len</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.055em">⋅</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝔾</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">len</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝔾</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">len</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.055em">⋅</mo><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">max</mtext></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝕏</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝔾</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">len</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.055em">⋅</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">max</mtext></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝕏</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝔾</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">len</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.055em">⋅</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝔾</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">len</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>PRM</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}L_{\text{GRPO}}({\mathbb{G}})&amp;=\frac{1}{\sum_{g^{(i)}\in{\mathbb{G}}}\textit{len}(g^{(i)})}\cdot\sum_{g^{(i)}\in{\mathbb{G}}}\sum_{t=0}^{\textit{len}(g^{(i)})-1}({P}_{i,t}\cdot{a}_{i})-{D}_{i,t}=\\
\frac{1}{\sum_{g^{(i)}\in{\mathbb{G}}}\textit{len}(g^{(i)})}\cdot\sum_{t=0}^{t_{\textit{max}}-1}&amp;\sum_{\lambda\in{\mathbb{X}}_{t}}\sum_{g^{(i)}\in\lambda}({P}_{i,t}\cdot{a}_{i})-{D}_{i,t}=\frac{1}{\sum_{g^{(i)}\in{\mathbb{G}}}\textit{len}(g^{(i)})}\cdot\sum_{t=0}^{t_{\textit{max}}-1}\sum_{\lambda\in{\mathbb{X}}_{t}}\sum_{g^{(i)}\in\lambda}({P}_{i,t}\cdot{A}_{i,t})-{D}_{i,t}\\
&amp;=\frac{1}{\sum_{g^{(i)}\in{\mathbb{G}}}\textit{len}(g^{(i)})}\cdot\sum_{g^{(i)}\in{\mathbb{G}}}\sum_{t=0}^{\textit{len}(g^{(i)})-1}({P}_{i,t}\cdot{A}_{i,t})-{D}_{i,t}=L_{\text{PRM}}({\mathbb{G}})\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p20.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p20.1.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p21" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p21.1" class="ltx_p">In other words, the standard GRPO objective defined in Equation <a href="#S2.E2.1" title="In 2 ‣ 2.1 GRPO ‣ 2 Background ‣ GRPO is Secretly a Process Reward Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2a</span></a> automatically induces the PRM and PRM-aware objective defined in Equations <a href="#S3.E3" title="In 3.1 Theoretical Analysis ‣ 3 The PRM in the Machine ‣ GRPO is Secretly a Process Reward Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>-<a href="#S3.E4" title="In 3.1 Theoretical Analysis ‣ 3 The PRM in the Machine ‣ GRPO is Secretly a Process Reward Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Empirical Analysis</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The theoretical analysis in Section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Theoretical Analysis ‣ 3 The PRM in the Machine ‣ GRPO is Secretly a Process Reward Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> shows that the GRPO objective induces a PRM: it remains to be shown, however, that this induced PRM is <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">non-trivial</span>. We refer to the set <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{B}}({\mathbb{G}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{B}}({\mathbb{G}})</annotation></semantics></math> of process sets as <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">trivial</span> if it contains only singleton sets<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
              
              
              
            And, by definition, <math id="footnote3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔾</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{G}}</annotation></semantics></math> itself, with <math id="footnote3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s({\mathbb{G}})=e({\mathbb{G}})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s({\mathbb{G}})=e({\mathbb{G}})=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></span>—i.e.  <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{B}}({\mathbb{G}})=\{{\mathbb{G}}\}\cup\{\{g^{(i)}\}\hskip 2.84526pt|\hskip 2.84526ptg^{(i)}\in{\mathbb{G}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>𝔾</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝔾</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{B}}({\mathbb{G}})=\{{\mathbb{G}}\}\cup\{\{g^{(i)}\}\hskip 2.84526pt|\hskip 2.84526ptg^{(i)}\in{\mathbb{G}}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{B}}({\mathbb{G}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{B}}({\mathbb{G}})</annotation></semantics></math> is trivial, then there are no meaningful process steps within each trajectory, and only the outcome reward has an effect on learning. On the other hand, if <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{B}}({\mathbb{G}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{B}}({\mathbb{G}})</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">not</span> trivial, then Theorem <a href="#Thmthm1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 3.1 Theoretical Analysis ‣ 3 The PRM in the Machine ‣ GRPO is Secretly a Process Reward Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> entails that the standard GRPO objective of Equation <a href="#S2.E2.1" title="In 2 ‣ 2.1 GRPO ‣ 2 Background ‣ GRPO is Secretly a Process Reward Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2a</span></a> induces a non-trivial PRM.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To analyze the complexity of real-world, GRPO-derived step-level rewards, we empirically evaluated <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{B}}({\mathbb{G}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{B}}({\mathbb{G}})</annotation></semantics></math> structures generated during standard GRPO training: for each group <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔾</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{G}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we computed its <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{B}}({\mathbb{G}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{B}}({\mathbb{G}})</annotation></semantics></math> tree, and measured the number of intermediate nodes between the root <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔾</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{G}}</annotation></semantics></math> and each terminal node <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{g^{(i)}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{g^{(i)}\}</annotation></semantics></math> (<span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">path depth</span>), as a proxy for the complexity of the <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{B}}({\mathbb{G}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{B}}({\mathbb{G}})</annotation></semantics></math> structure.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In addition, for each completion <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{(i)}\in{\mathbb{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝔾</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{(i)}\in{\mathbb{G}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we counted the number of tokens <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{(i)}_{\textit{term}}=e(\{g^{(i)}\})-s(\{g^{(i)}\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">term</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{(i)}_{\textit{term}}=e(\{g^{(i)}\})-s(\{g^{(i)}\})</annotation></semantics></math> contained in the process step corresponding to the terminal node <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{g^{(i)}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{g^{(i)}\}</annotation></semantics></math>—i.e. the number of tokens unique to <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math>—and calculated the <span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">intermediate proportion</span> <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{p}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{p}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{p}_{i}=(\textit{len}(g^{(i)})-n^{(i)}_{\textit{term}})/\textit{len}(g^{(i)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">len</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">term</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">len</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{p}_{i}=(\textit{len}(g^{(i)})-n^{(i)}_{\textit{term}})/\textit{len}(g^{(i)})</annotation></semantics></math>. Higher values of <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{p}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{p}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> indicate that a greater proportion of the trajectory <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to intermediate process steps and is therefore assigned non-trivial step-level reward.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.21154v1/exp0_res.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/300;" width="548" height="300" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Validation reward (exact-match accuracy; left), <math id="S3.F2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{B}}({\mathbb{G}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{B}}({\mathbb{G}})</annotation></semantics></math> root-to-terminal path depth (center), and proportions of trajectories spanned by intermediate (non-terminal) process steps (right) for GRPO runs with group sizes of 36 (top) and 6 (bottom).</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Experimental Setup.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We trained two DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-1.5B models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">DeepSeek-AI, 2025</a>)</cite> on the OpenRS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dang &amp; Ngo, 2025</a>)</cite> dataset using the standard GRPO algorithm and objective of Equation <a href="#S2.E2" title="In 2.1 GRPO ‣ 2 Background ‣ GRPO is Secretly a Process Reward Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. We selected 125 OpenRS examples at random to serve as a validation set.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The first model trained for 1675 steps with a group size of six and a learn rate of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6\times 10^{-6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6\times 10^{-6}</annotation></semantics></math>. The second was trained with a group size of 36 and a learn rate of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-6}</annotation></semantics></math> for 275 steps (due to the larger group size). Both models were trained with a maximum new token limit of 4096, a batch size of four, and a temperature of 0.75. Additional training details are located in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Experimental Setup ‣ GRPO is Secretly a Process Reward Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Results.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3.2 Empirical Analysis ‣ 3 The PRM in the Machine ‣ GRPO is Secretly a Process Reward Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows that both path depth and intermediate proportion increase drastically as validation reward saturates, for group sizes of six and 36. These results are supported by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al. (2025)</a></cite>, who find that entropy decreases sharply as GRPO training progresses: this indicates that increasingly rich PRM-inducing structures arise as the model converges on a locally optimal policy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In addition, found that only twelve of 6,700 <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{B}}({\mathbb{G}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{B}}({\mathbb{G}})</annotation></semantics></math> structures were trivial with a group size of six (<math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>0.2%). With a group size of 36, zero trivial <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{B}}({\mathbb{G}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{B}}({\mathbb{G}})</annotation></semantics></math> structures arose out of the 1,100 generated groups. Examples of non-trivial <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{B}}({\mathbb{G}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{B}}({\mathbb{G}})</annotation></semantics></math> structures from this experiment are given in Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C ℬ(𝔾) Structure Examples ‣ GRPO is Secretly a Process Reward Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In conjunction with the theoretical analysis of Section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Theoretical Analysis ‣ 3 The PRM in the Machine ‣ GRPO is Secretly a Process Reward Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, the results of this experiment demonstrate that GRPO induces a non-trivial PRM under real-world conditions. In Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Proposed Approach: 𝜆-GRPO ‣ GRPO is Secretly a Process Reward Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we show that this induced PRM carries a serious flaw that is detrimental to RL training, and propose a minor modification to the GRPO algorithm to mitigate this shortcoming.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Proposed Approach: <math id="S4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>-GRPO</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Viewing the GRPO objective in terms of process set partitions <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕏</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> (see Equation <a href="#S3.E6" title="In Proof. ‣ 3.1 Theoretical Analysis ‣ 3 The PRM in the Machine ‣ GRPO is Secretly a Process Reward Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>), we note that the contribution of each trajectory <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{(i)}\in{\mathbb{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝔾</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{(i)}\in{\mathbb{G}}</annotation></semantics></math> to the loss at index <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is identical to that of all other trajectories in the process set <math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda^{(i,t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda^{(i,t)}</annotation></semantics></math> (where <math id="S4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{P}_{t}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{P}_{t}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{D}_{t}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{D}_{t}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{A}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{A}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> are defined as in Equation <a href="#S3.E5" title="In Proof. ‣ 3.1 Theoretical Analysis ‣ 3 The PRM in the Machine ‣ GRPO is Secretly a Process Reward Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>):</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S4.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\frac{1}{\sum_{g^{(i)}\in{\mathbb{G}}}\textit{len}(g^{(i)})}\cdot\sum_{g^{(i)}\in{\mathbb{G}}}&amp;\sum_{t=0}^{\textit{len}(g^{(i)})-1}({P}_{i,t}\cdot{a}_{i})-{D}_{i,t}=\frac{1}{\sum_{g^{(i)}\in{\mathbb{G}}}\textit{len}(g^{(i)})}\cdot\sum_{t=0}^{t_{\textit{max}}-1}\sum_{\lambda\in{\mathbb{X}}_{t}}\sum_{g^{(i)}\in\lambda}({P}_{i,t}\cdot{A}_{i,t})-{D}_{i,t}\\
=&amp;\frac{1}{\sum_{g^{(i)}\in{\mathbb{G}}}\textit{len}(g^{(i)})}\cdot\sum_{t=0}^{t_{\textit{max}}-1}\sum_{\lambda\in{\mathbb{X}}_{t}}|\lambda|\cdot((\hat{P}_{t}(\lambda)\cdot\hat{A}(\lambda))-\hat{D}_{t}(\lambda))\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝔾</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">len</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.055em">⋅</mo><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝔾</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">len</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝔾</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">len</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.055em">⋅</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">max</mtext></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝕏</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝔾</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">len</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.055em">⋅</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">max</mtext></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝕏</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\frac{1}{\sum_{g^{(i)}\in{\mathbb{G}}}\textit{len}(g^{(i)})}\cdot\sum_{g^{(i)}\in{\mathbb{G}}}&amp;\sum_{t=0}^{\textit{len}(g^{(i)})-1}({P}_{i,t}\cdot{a}_{i})-{D}_{i,t}=\frac{1}{\sum_{g^{(i)}\in{\mathbb{G}}}\textit{len}(g^{(i)})}\cdot\sum_{t=0}^{t_{\textit{max}}-1}\sum_{\lambda\in{\mathbb{X}}_{t}}\sum_{g^{(i)}\in\lambda}({P}_{i,t}\cdot{A}_{i,t})-{D}_{i,t}\\
=&amp;\frac{1}{\sum_{g^{(i)}\in{\mathbb{G}}}\textit{len}(g^{(i)})}\cdot\sum_{t=0}^{t_{\textit{max}}-1}\sum_{\lambda\in{\mathbb{X}}_{t}}|\lambda|\cdot((\hat{P}_{t}(\lambda)\cdot\hat{A}(\lambda))-\hat{D}_{t}(\lambda))\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The contribution of each process set <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in{\mathcal{B}}({\mathbb{G}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in{\mathcal{B}}({\mathbb{G}})</annotation></semantics></math> to the overall loss, <math id="S4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{P}_{t}(\lambda)\cdot\hat{A}(\lambda)-\hat{D}_{t}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{P}_{t}(\lambda)\cdot\hat{A}(\lambda)-\hat{D}_{t}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math>, is scaled by <math id="S4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\lambda|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\lambda|</annotation></semantics></math>: this carries the danger of harming exploration (for <math id="S4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{A}(\lambda)&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{A}(\lambda)&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>) <span id="S4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and</span> exploitation (for <math id="S4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{A}(\lambda)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{A}(\lambda)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>). Consider some process set <math id="S4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\lambda|\gg 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≫</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\lambda|\gg 1</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{A}(\lambda)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{A}(\lambda)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, then the increase in probability assigned to the process step corresponding to <math id="S4.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S4.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> under GRPO is compounded by a factor of <math id="S4.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\lambda|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\lambda|</annotation></semantics></math>, decreasing the likelihood of exploring process steps that are dissimilar from <math id="S4.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> in subsequent training episodes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Conversely, if <math id="S4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{A}(\lambda)&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{A}(\lambda)&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, then the <span id="S4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">decrease</span> in probability assigned to <math id="S4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> under GRPO is compounded by a factor of <math id="S4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\lambda|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\lambda|</annotation></semantics></math>, decreasing the likelihood of exploiting high-reward trajectories in <math id="S4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>. To illustrate, consider the group <math id="S4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔾</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{G}}</annotation></semantics></math> in Figure <a href="#footnote2" title="footnote 2 ‣ Figure 1 ‣ 3 The PRM in the Machine ‣ GRPO is Secretly a Process Reward Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, assume <math id="S4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{r}_{1}={r}_{2}={r}_{6}=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{r}_{1}={r}_{2}={r}_{6}=0.5</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{r}_{4}={r}_{5}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{r}_{4}={r}_{5}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{r}_{3}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{r}_{3}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S4.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=\{g^{(3)},g^{(4)},g^{(5)}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=\{g^{(3)},g^{(4)},g^{(5)}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S4.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{A}(\lambda)=-0.22" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.22</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{A}(\lambda)=-0.22</annotation></semantics></math>: despite the fact that <math id="S4.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math> has the highest reward, the probability of the sub-trajectory <math id="S4.p4.m12" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="g^{(3)}_{:4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{(3)}_{:4}</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S4.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">decreased</span> under the GRPO objective, thereby decreasing the overall likelihood of generating the completion <math id="S4.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{(3)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{(3)}</annotation></semantics></math>. The term <math id="S4.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\lambda|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\lambda|</annotation></semantics></math> in Equation <a href="#S4.E7" title="In 4 Proposed Approach: 𝜆-GRPO ‣ GRPO is Secretly a Process Reward Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> then scales this decrease in probability by a factor of three.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We propose scaling the token-level loss for <math id="S4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{(i)}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{(i)}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\lambda^{(i,t)}|^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\lambda^{(i,t)}|^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>-GRPO; Equation <a href="#S4.E8" title="In 4 Proposed Approach: 𝜆-GRPO ‣ GRPO is Secretly a Process Reward Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>): this has the effect of canceling out the term <math id="S4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\lambda|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\lambda|</annotation></semantics></math> in Equation <a href="#S4.E7" title="In 4 Proposed Approach: 𝜆-GRPO ‣ GRPO is Secretly a Process Reward Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, so that each process set contributes equally to the loss at index <math id="S4.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S4.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}L_{\lambda\text{-GRPO}}({\mathbb{G}})&amp;=\frac{1}{\sum_{g^{(i)}\in{\mathbb{G}}}\textit{len}(g^{(i)})}\cdot\sum_{g^{(i)}\in{\mathbb{G}}}\sum_{t=0}^{\textit{len}(g^{(i)})-1}\frac{({P}_{i,t}\cdot{a}_{i})-{D}_{i,t}}{|\lambda^{(i,t)}|}\\
&amp;=\frac{1}{\sum_{g^{(i)}\in{\mathbb{G}}}\textit{len}(g^{(i)})}\cdot\sum_{t=0}^{t_{\textit{max}}-1}\sum_{\lambda\in{\mathbb{X}}_{t}}(\hat{P}_{t}(\lambda)\cdot\hat{A}(\lambda))-\hat{D}_{t}(\lambda)\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-GRPO</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝔾</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">len</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.055em">⋅</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝔾</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">len</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝔾</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">len</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.055em">⋅</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">max</mtext></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝕏</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}L_{\lambda\text{-GRPO}}({\mathbb{G}})&amp;=\frac{1}{\sum_{g^{(i)}\in{\mathbb{G}}}\textit{len}(g^{(i)})}\cdot\sum_{g^{(i)}\in{\mathbb{G}}}\sum_{t=0}^{\textit{len}(g^{(i)})-1}\frac{({P}_{i,t}\cdot{a}_{i})-{D}_{i,t}}{|\lambda^{(i,t)}|}\\
&amp;=\frac{1}{\sum_{g^{(i)}\in{\mathbb{G}}}\textit{len}(g^{(i)})}\cdot\sum_{t=0}^{t_{\textit{max}}-1}\sum_{\lambda\in{\mathbb{X}}_{t}}(\hat{P}_{t}(\lambda)\cdot\hat{A}(\lambda))-\hat{D}_{t}(\lambda)\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate our proposed approach, we trained DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-1.5B and Llama-3.2-1B-Instruct<span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
            
            
            
          <a href="https://huggingface.co/meta-llama/Llama-3.2-1B-Instruct" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">https://huggingface.co/meta-llama/Llama-3.2-1B-Instruct</a></span></span></span> with the <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>-GRPO (Equation <a href="#S4.E8" title="In 4 Proposed Approach: 𝜆-GRPO ‣ GRPO is Secretly a Process Reward Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>) objective on the OpenRS dataset of Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Empirical Analysis ‣ 3 The PRM in the Machine ‣ GRPO is Secretly a Process Reward Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, and compared them to standard GRPO (Equation <a href="#S2.E2.1" title="In 2 ‣ 2.1 GRPO ‣ 2 Background ‣ GRPO is Secretly a Process Reward Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2a</span></a>) models trained with an identical setup. All models were evaluated on an OpenRS validation set and five downstream reasoning benchmarks (see Section <a href="#S5.SS1" title="5.1 Experimental Setup ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ GRPO is Secretly a Process Reward Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Experimental Setup</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All models were trained for 1,000 steps with a group size of six, a batch size of four, a maximum of 4,096 new tokens, and a temperature of 0.75. We conducted two sets of trials across the two models (for a total of four trials): in the first, we set the KL coefficient <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=0.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=0.0</annotation></semantics></math>, and in the second <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=0.04" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.04</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=0.04</annotation></semantics></math>. The Qwen models were trained with a learn rate of <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-6}</annotation></semantics></math>; the Llama models were trained with a learn rate of <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\times 10^{-7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\times 10^{-7}</annotation></semantics></math> for the <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=0.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=0.0</annotation></semantics></math> trial and <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-7}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=0.04" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.04</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=0.04</annotation></semantics></math> (as training was highly unstable with higher learn rates for Llama). Additional training details are located in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Experimental Setup ‣ GRPO is Secretly a Process Reward Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluated the models on the AIME24<span id="footnote5" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">5</span>
              
              
              
            <a href="https://huggingface.co/datasets/AI-MO/aimo-validation-aime" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">https://huggingface.co/datasets/AI-MO/aimo-validation-aime</a></span></span></span>, MATH-500 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hendrycks et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lightman et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, AMC23<span id="footnote6" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">6</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">6</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">6</span>
              
              
              
            <a href="https://huggingface.co/datasets/AI-MO/aimo-validation-amc" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">https://huggingface.co/datasets/AI-MO/aimo-validation-amc</a></span></span></span>, Minerva <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lewkowycz et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, and OlympiadBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al., 2024</a>)</cite> benchmarks, using the LightEval framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Habib et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dang &amp; Ngo (2025)</a></cite>. All models were evaluated at the checkpoint corresponding to the step at which they achieved maximum validation accuracy. As in the experiment in Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Empirical Analysis ‣ 3 The PRM in the Machine ‣ GRPO is Secretly a Process Reward Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, we withheld 125 examples as a validation set.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Results and Discussion</h3>

<figure id="S5.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.21154v1/rwd_curves.png" id="S5.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/112;" width="548" height="112" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Models’ validation accuracy across training steps. Peak accuracy is highlighted by vertical, dashed lines.</figcaption>
